    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin-top:0;">Full schedule: <a href="#">https://your-site/schedule</a></p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 id="blackout">Blackout dates</h2>
<p>Already know a date won't work this season? Go to <a href="/blackout">Blackout Dates</a>, pick your name, and check off every date you can't play — it saves instantly, no confirmation step. This only works before the admin generates the schedule; once it exists, blackout dates lock and you'll use <strong>Request a Sub</strong> or <strong>Swap a Week</strong> instead.</p>
<p>Enrolled in more than one session with a match on the same date? Enter the blackout date once — it carries over automatically and shows up already checked (greyed out) on the other session. Want to see every blackout date you've ever entered, across every session at once, not just this one? Check "Show all my blackout dates" right under the name dropdown.</p>
<p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:0;">You'll also get an email when a new session opens up for blackout dates, so you don't have to go looking:</p>
<div class="email-mock">
  <div class="email-mock-header">
    <div class="to">To: jamie@example.com</div>
    <div class="subject">Riverside Tennis Club — Enter your blackout dates — Wednesday Doubles, 6:00 PM, Court 2</div>
  </div>
  <div class="email-mock-body">
    <div class="email-mock-banner" style="background:#0969da1a;border-left:5px solid #0969da;">
      <div class="name">Wednesday Doubles — Riverside Tennis Club, Court 2</div>
      <div class="date">6:00 PM, Court 2</div>
    </div>
    <p>Hi Jamie Rivera,</p>
    <p><strong>Wednesday Doubles</strong> is being scheduled. If there are any dates you already know you can't play, let us know before the schedule is generated:</p>
    <p><a href="#" class="btn-confirm">Enter your blackout dates</a></p>
    <p>That link will already have your name selected &mdash; just check off any dates you can't make. Nothing else to do if you don't have any &mdash; you'll be assumed available every week.</p>
    <p>This only works until the schedule is generated &mdash; after that, use Request a Sub instead for any date you end up needing to miss.</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin:0 0 4px;">Riverside Tennis Club &mdash; Court 2</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin-top:0;">Full schedule: <a href="#">https://your-site/schedule</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:0;">The button takes you straight here, with your name already selected:</p>
<img class="screenshot" src="/img/help/blackout.png?v=<%= assetVersion %>" alt="Blackout Dates page showing the session switcher banner, a name dropdown, and a checklist of match dates.">
<p class="screenshot-caption">Pick your name, check off the dates you can't make, and save — that's the whole page.</p>

<h2 id="request-sub">Request a Sub</h2>
<p>Know ahead of time you'll miss a scheduled match? Go to <a href="/request-sub">Request a Sub</a>, pick your name, and click "Need a sub for this week" next to the date — no need to wait for the reminder email. Since this page has no login, clicking the button doesn't email anyone else right away: you'll get a confirmation email first, and only once you click the link in it does everyone not already playing that week get an offer — first to respond gets the spot. That confirmation email names exactly who just got asked, when it escalates to the sub list if nobody responds, and what to do if nobody ever confirms — so you always know where things stand without having to check back. A red "double booked" badge next to a date is usually the one worth requesting a sub for — see below.</p>
<p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:0;">What the offer looks like to everyone else on the roster:</p>
<div class="email-mock">
  <div class="email-mock-header">
    <div class="to">To: dan@example.com</div>
    <div class="subject">Riverside Tennis Club — Sub needed — Wednesday, Sep 2, 6:00 PM, Court 2 doubles</div>
  </div>
  <div class="email-mock-body">
    <div class="email-mock-banner" style="background:#0969da1a;border-left:5px solid #0969da;">
      <div class="name">Wednesday Doubles — Riverside Tennis Club, Court 2</div>
      <div class="date">Wednesday, Sep 2 &middot; 6:00 PM, Court 2</div>
    </div>
    <p>Hi Dan Ostrow,</p>
    <p>Jamie Rivera needs a sub for <strong>Wednesday, Sep 2</strong> at 6:00 PM. First to confirm gets the spot.</p>
    <p><a href="#" class="btn-confirm">I'll play</a></p>
    <p><strong>Playing that week:</strong> Alice Chen, Marcus Lee, Priya Nair<br><strong>Bringing balls:</strong> Alice Chen</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin:0 0 4px;">Riverside Tennis Club &mdash; Court 2</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin-top:0;">Full schedule: <a href="#">https://your-site/schedule</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
<img class="screenshot" src="/img/help/request-sub.png?v=<%= assetVersion %>" alt="Request a Sub page listing every upcoming week with a status badge and a red 'Need a sub for this week' button next to each date.">
<p class="screenshot-caption">A red <span class="badge double_booked">double booked</span> badge next to a date is a strong hint that's the one worth requesting a sub for.</p>
<p class="muted">Once you've requested a sub for a week, the button there is replaced with a note that it's already out; once someone claims it, that week shows <span class="badge subbed_out">subbed out</span> instead. Nothing to click either way — it's just there so you can check it worked.</p>

<h2 id="swap">Swap a Week</h2>
<p>Prefer to trade with one specific person instead of opening it to the whole roster? Go to <a href="/swap">Swap a Week</a>, pick your name, choose which of your own weeks to give up, then pick who to swap with and which of their weeks you want instead. Since this page has no login, submitting doesn't email the other player right away: you'll get a confirmation email first, and only once you click the link in it does the proposal actually go out to them — nothing changes until they accept. You're both still playing the same number of games, just different dates, so neither of you counts as a sub.</p>
<p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:0;">Here's what the other player sees:</p>
<div class="email-mock">
  <div class="email-mock-header">
    <div class="to">To: alice@example.com</div>
    <div class="subject">Riverside Tennis Club — Jamie Rivera wants to swap weeks with you — Wednesday Doubles, 6:00 PM, Court 2</div>
  </div>
  <div class="email-mock-body">
    <div class="email-mock-banner" style="background:#0969da1a;border-left:5px solid #0969da;">
      <div class="name">Wednesday Doubles — Riverside Tennis Club, Court 2</div>
      <div class="date">6:00 PM, Court 2</div>
    </div>
    <p>Hi Alice Chen,</p>
    <p>Jamie Rivera would like to swap with you in <strong>Wednesday Doubles</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>You'd give up <strong>Wednesday, Sep 9</strong></li>
      <li>You'd take over <strong>Wednesday, Sep 2</strong> (currently Jamie's)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You're still playing the same number of games either way &mdash; just trading which week.</p>
    <p><a href="#" class="btn-confirm">Review and respond</a></p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin:0 0 4px;">Riverside Tennis Club &mdash; Court 2</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin-top:0;">Full schedule: <a href="#">https://your-site/schedule</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
<img class="screenshot" src="/img/help/swap.png?v=<%= assetVersion %>" alt="Swap a Week page showing 'Which week do you want to give up?' with a table of dates and a 'Give up this week' button next to each.">
<p class="screenshot-caption">Step one: pick which of your own weeks you're offering up. The next page lets you pick who you want to swap with.</p>

<h2 id="timing">The full timeline: what happens, and when</h2>
<p>The sections above cover each page on its own — this one's the big picture: exactly when each automatic email fires, and what happens if you never respond to one. Three flows, one for each of confirming, requesting a sub, and swapping.</p>

<h3>Confirming you're playing</h3>
<div class="flowchart">
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-when">2 days before your match (the admin can change this)</div>
    <div class="flow-title">Reminder email sent</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Confirm and Need a sub buttons. Sent once per week, per player.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-when">~27 hours before match time (the admin can change this)</div>
    <div class="flow-title">Still haven't responded? One follow-up nudge</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Same two buttons. Only goes out if you're still sitting on "scheduled" at that point — skipped entirely if you already confirmed or asked for a sub. Timed to land the day before, not the morning of, so it's more likely to reach you during work hours for an evening match.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-branches">
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">You click Confirm</div>
      <div class="flow-step success">
        <div class="flow-title">You're confirmed</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">Nothing else to do.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">You click Need a sub</div>
      <div class="flow-step">
        <div class="flow-title">Jumps to Request a Sub</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">See that flow below — an email goes out to the rest of the roster.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">You do nothing</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step urgent">
        <div class="flow-when">Match time arrives</div>
        <div class="flow-title">Nothing else happens automatically</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">There's no extra nudge or escalation the way there is for a sub request — those two emails are it. Your status just stays "scheduled" for good, the week locks, and both links stop working. The admin sees an "unconfirmed" flag on their dashboard, but from there it's a manual call, not anything the app does on its own.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3 id="flow-sub">Request a Sub</h3>
<div class="flowchart">
  <div class="flow-branches">
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">From a reminder email</div>
      <div class="flow-step">
        <div class="flow-title">Click "Need a sub"</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">The link itself proves it's really you, so this goes straight to the confirm page below.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">From the Request a Sub page</div>
      <div class="flow-step">
        <div class="flow-title">Pick your week — no link yet</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">You'll get a verification email first. Nothing goes out to anyone else until you click the link in it — that's what stops someone else mashing the button on your behalf.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step pending">
    <div class="flow-title">"Are you sure?"</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">One more click — this is the point of no return, since it emails the rest of the roster right away.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-title">Everyone not already playing that week gets an email</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Except anyone already blacked out on that date. First to click "I'll play" gets the spot.</div>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-note">An admin can also flag a slot as needing a sub on your behalf, without you asking. It follows the same steps from here on — the only difference is the email to the rest of the roster waits until this week's normal reminder time instead of going out right away.</p>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-branches">
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">Someone claims it</div>
      <div class="flow-step success">
        <div class="flow-title">Spot filled</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">You're marked subbed out and get your own "you're all set" email, they're confirmed, and everyone else still playing that week gets notified too. Done.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">Nobody claims it yet</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step">
        <div class="flow-when">24 hours before match</div>
        <div class="flow-title">Escalates to the sub list</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">A separate, admin-managed pool of subs gets an email too (skipped if the session has none assigned).</div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-branches">
        <div class="flow-branch">
          <div class="flow-branch-label">A sub claims it</div>
          <div class="flow-step success">
            <div class="flow-title">Spot filled</div>
            <div class="flow-detail">Same as above. Done.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-branch">
          <div class="flow-branch-label">Still nobody</div>
          <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
          <div class="flow-step urgent">
            <div class="flow-when">Match time arrives</div>
            <div class="flow-title">Marked unfilled</div>
            <div class="flow-detail">Flagged for the admin. From here it's their call — add someone manually, or the week plays a player short.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-note">An admin can close this out at any point along the way — reassigning the slot, marking you confirmed after all, or clearing the request entirely if you picked the wrong week by mistake (that puts you back to "scheduled" so you're reminded normally again).</p>
</div>

<h3>Swap a Week</h3>
<div class="flowchart">
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-title">You propose a trade</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Pick one of your own upcoming weeks, a specific teammate, and one of their weeks you'd rather play instead — no link yet, since this page has no login.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step pending">
    <div class="flow-title">Confirm it's you</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">A verification email goes to you first. Nothing goes out to the other player until you click the link in it — that's what stops someone else proposing a swap on your behalf.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-title">They get an email to accept or decline</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Nothing changes until they respond — you'll get a confirmation that the proposal went out.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-branches">
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">They accept</div>
      <div class="flow-step success">
        <div class="flow-title">Swap complete</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">Your slots trade places, both go straight to confirmed, and everyone else in both affected weeks gets a heads up. Done.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">They decline</div>
      <div class="flow-step urgent">
        <div class="flow-title">Nothing changes</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">You're notified. Your original week is still yours.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">They don't respond yet</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step">
        <div class="flow-when">48 hours before the earlier of the two match dates</div>
        <div class="flow-title">One nudge email</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">Sent to them only — you already know you're waiting.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-branches">
        <div class="flow-branch">
          <div class="flow-branch-label">They respond</div>
          <div class="flow-step">
            <div class="flow-title">Accept or decline</div>
            <div class="flow-detail">Same outcomes as above.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-branch">
          <div class="flow-branch-label">Still nothing</div>
          <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
          <div class="flow-step urgent">
            <div class="flow-when">The earlier match date arrives</div>
            <div class="flow-title">Proposal expires</div>
            <div class="flow-detail">The trade can't happen anymore — that week's about to lock. No email; it quietly closes out so your weeks are free to swap with someone else.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-note">An admin can cancel a pending swap directly. Reassigning either side's slot while a swap is still pending automatically cancels it too.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="my-page">My Page</h2>
<p>The fastest way to see everything at once. Go to <a href="/me">My Page</a>, pick your name, and bookmark the link (or save it to your phone's home screen) — it always shows your current schedule across every session you're in, no need to pick your name again. Request a sub or propose a swap with one click on any upcoming match, and find your calendar-subscribe link here too. Each session's card also shows a quick target/played/ball-duty line for that session, and — if you've entered a blackout date that lands on one of that session's own match weeks — a note right above the card naming the date.</p>
<img class="screenshot" src="/img/help/my-page.png?v=<%= assetVersion %>" alt="My Page showing one colored card per enrolled session, each with a table of upcoming matches, status badges, and Need a sub / Propose a swap buttons.">
<p class="screenshot-caption">Enrolled in more than one session? Each one gets its own colored card here — same color you'll see everywhere else that session shows up.</p>

<h2 id="schedule">Season Schedule &amp; Next 4 Weeks</h2>
<p><a href="/schedule">Season Schedule</a> shows the whole season at a glance — every date, who's playing, how they're paired up, and who's on ball duty. <a href="/lookahead">Next 4 Weeks</a> is the same table trimmed to just what's coming up soon.</p>
<img class="screenshot" src="/img/help/next-four-weeks.png?v=<%= assetVersion %>" alt="Next 4 Weeks page showing a table with Date, Team A, Team B, and Ball duty columns for the four upcoming match dates.">
<p class="screenshot-caption">Team A and Team B for each date, plus who's bringing the balls that week.</p>

<h2 id="calendar">Calendar</h2>
<p>Go to <a href="/calendar">Calendar</a> to add your matches to your phone or computer, two ways: a one-time download for the current season, or <strong>Subscribe</strong>, which stays up to date automatically as your schedule changes — the better choice if you don't want to think about it again.</p>
<img class="screenshot" src="/img/help/calendar.png?v=<%= assetVersion %>" alt="Personal Calendar page with two sections: a one-time .ics download and a Subscribe option with a webcal link.">
<p class="screenshot-caption">Subscribe once and re-schedules, subs, and swaps show up automatically — no need to come back and re-download.</p>

<h2 id="pdf">Print Schedule</h2>
<p><a href="/pdf">Print Schedule</a> gives you a single printable page with the whole season's schedule on it — handy to print and stick on the fridge or pass around at a gathering. If more than one session is currently active, there's also a "Download All Active Sessions" option that combines every session into one PDF sorted by date instead of session-by-session — a couple of pages instead of one, but easier to check "what's happening this week" across everything at once.</p>

<h2 id="adhoc">Pickup games (ad-hoc sessions)</h2>
<p>Some sessions are pickup games instead of a fixed season schedule — filled week to week, first come first served. None of the sections above apply here: no season roster, no target games, no blackout dates, no confirm/need-a-sub flow. Signing up <em>is</em> your confirmation.</p>
<p>An invite goes out a couple of days before each match. The first 4 to sign up get the first court, the next 4 get a second, and so on — no cap on how many courts can fill. Nothing's held for you, so sign up before someone else takes the spot.</p>
<p class="muted" style="margin-bottom:0;">The invite looks like this:</p>
<div class="email-mock">
  <div class="email-mock-header">
    <div class="to">To: dan@example.com</div>
    <div class="subject">Riverside Tennis Club — Pickup game Thursday, Sep 4, 6:00 PM, Court 4 — want in?</div>
  </div>
  <div class="email-mock-body">
    <div class="email-mock-banner" style="background:#0969da1a;border-left:5px solid #0969da;">
      <div class="name">Thursday Pickup — Riverside Tennis Club, Court 4</div>
      <div class="date">Thursday, Sep 4 &middot; 6:00 PM, Court 4</div>
    </div>
    <p>Hi Dan Ostrow,</p>
    <p>Looking for players for <strong>Thursday, Sep 4</strong> at 6:00 PM. First come, first served — the first 4 to sign up get the first court, the next 4 get a second court, and so on.</p>
    <p><a href="#" class="btn-confirm">I'm in</a></p>
    <p>If you're not free this time, no need to do anything — you'll get invited again for the next one.</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin:0 0 4px;">Riverside Tennis Club &mdash; Court 4</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin-top:0;">Full schedule: <a href="#">https://your-site/schedule</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>If sign-ups aren't a clean multiple of 4 as match day nears, anyone who hasn't signed up yet may get one short reminder asking if they're still in. Once it's decided — usually about a day before — you'll get one of two follow-ups. Landed on a court? A note telling you who you're playing with:</p>
<div class="email-mock">
  <div class="email-mock-header">
    <div class="to">To: dan@example.com</div>
    <div class="subject">Riverside Tennis Club — You're in — Thursday, Sep 4, 6:00 PM, Court 4</div>
  </div>
  <div class="email-mock-body">
    <div class="email-mock-banner" style="background:#0969da1a;border-left:5px solid #0969da;">
      <div class="name">Thursday Pickup — Riverside Tennis Club, Court 4</div>
      <div class="date">Thursday, Sep 4 &middot; 6:00 PM, Court 4</div>
    </div>
    <p>Hi Dan Ostrow,</p>
    <p>You're set for <strong>Thursday, Sep 4</strong> at 6:00 PM, Court 4.</p>
    <p><strong>Playing with:</strong> Alice Chen, Marcus Lee, Priya Nair</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin:0 0 4px;">Riverside Tennis Club &mdash; Court 4</p>
    <p class="email-mock-footer" style="margin-top:0;">Full schedule: <a href="#">https://your-site/schedule</a></p>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="muted">— or, if your group never reached 4, a short note that this one didn't come together. Either way, there's nothing more to do; you'll be invited again for the next pickup game.</p>

<p>Here's the whole thing as one picture:</p>
<div class="flowchart">
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-when">~56 hours before match (the admin can change this)</div>
    <div class="flow-title">Invite email sent to everyone on the roster</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">One "I'm in" link each. First come, first served — no waiting for a deadline, a court forms the moment 4 people have signed up.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-title">Sign-ups come in</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Every complete group of 4, in the order people signed up, becomes its own court — no cap on how many courts can fill.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-branches">
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">Sign-ups land on a clean multiple of 4</div>
      <div class="flow-step success">
        <div class="flow-title">No reminder needed</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">Every court's already full.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">There's a leftover group of 1-3</div>
      <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
      <div class="flow-step pending">
        <div class="flow-when">~30 hours before match (the admin can change this)</div>
        <div class="flow-title">One reminder — to stragglers only</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">Only sent to whoever on the roster hasn't signed up yet, not to anyone already on a full court.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-step">
    <div class="flow-when">~24 hours before match (the admin can change this)</div>
    <div class="flow-title">Courts finalize</div>
    <div class="flow-detail">Whatever's signed up at this point locks in — this is also your only confirmation, there's no separate confirm step.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="flow-arrow">&#8595;</div>
  <div class="flow-branches">
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">You landed on a full court</div>
      <div class="flow-step success">
        <div class="flow-title">"You're in" email</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">Names your teammates and court. Done.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-branch">
      <div class="flow-branch-label">Your group never reached 4</div>
      <div class="flow-step urgent">
        <div class="flow-title">"Not enough signed up" email</div>
        <div class="flow-detail">This one's not happening. Nothing else to do — you'll be invited again for the next pickup game.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="flow-note">Signing up doesn't lock in the way confirming does elsewhere on this site — there's no self-service way to back out once you've signed up. If your plans change, contact your admin directly; they can add or remove players on a pickup week by hand.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="double-booked">What does "double booked" mean?</h2>
<p>Enrolled in two sessions with matches on the same date? You'll see a red <span class="badge double_booked">double booked</span> badge in place of your usual status everywhere you'd check your schedule — the season schedule, My Page, Request a Sub, Swap a Week, the printable schedule, even the calendar invite. It doesn't fix itself — pick which one you're actually playing, then request a sub or propose a swap for the other, ideally weeks before match day rather than the week of. Once a sub has actually been requested for that slot, the badge switches to <span class="badge needs_sub">needs sub</span> instead, since that's the more useful thing to know at that point.</p>

<h2 id="statuses">What do the badges mean?</h2>
<table class="wide">
  <thead><tr><th>Badge</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><span class="badge scheduled">scheduled</span></td><td>You're down to play this week — you haven't confirmed yet, and you haven't been asked to.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge confirmed">confirmed</span></td><td>You've confirmed you're playing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge needs_sub">needs sub</span></td><td>A sub was requested for this slot and it hasn't been filled yet.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge subbed_out">subbed out</span></td><td>Someone else took this spot — you're not playing this week after all.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge double_booked">double booked</span></td><td>You're scheduled to play in two different sessions on this same date — see above.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

